title: Keybinding variables
tags: Keybindings Configuration
kind: reference
summary: A letter named once in keybindings.toml: variables in the keys, and the three target prefixes among them.

The shipped keys are built from a few letters with a role — a domain such as `,`
for the tags, a verb such as `a` for ''add'', a target prefix such as `i` for
''here''. `gui/keybindings.toml` names each of those letters once, so that moving
a role to another letter is one line, not fifty.

!! Variables

The table `vars` maps a name to keys, and `{name}` in a key of the file stands for
them; a list gives a variable several ways to be typed:

```
vars.tags = ","
vars.add = "a"
vars.bulk-query = "m q"
vars.delete = ["d", "x"]

"{tags} {add}" = { command = "mf:tag add" }
"{bulk-query} d" = { command = "metarecord:bulk query delete" }
"shift+{add}" = { command = "…" }
```

* A variable holds a key or a sequence (`"m q"`); its name is lowercase letters, digits and `-`.
* A variable with several values binds a key for each, on every line naming it: with the above, `"{bulk-query} {delete}"` binds `m q d` and `m q x`. A key naming two such variables binds every combination; a variable named twice in a key takes the same value in both places (`"{record} {record} s"` binds `m m s`, never `m z s`). To give one command another key, write it a line of its own: a variable is the same for all its lines.
* A list cannot be empty nor hold one key twice, and the error names the variable. A key bound twice through a value is refused like any other, and the error names the key, the lines and the variables involved.
* A value cannot name another variable: a key names its variables one by one, `"{record} {query-verb} {delete}"`, so that it reads as what is typed.
* The table can be written as `vars.name = …` lines anywhere at the top of the file, or as a `[vars]` section at its end — TOML puts every key after a section header into that section.
* A `{name}` that is not a variable is an error, which names it. A brace that names nothing (`"{"`) is a key like any other.
* Variables are replaced in the keys only, not in the commands.

!! Targets

Many commands take a target — where a panel opens: `here`, `other` (the other
slot) or `new` (a new workspace). The shipped file gives each such command four
lines: its own key, and the same under the prefixes `target-here`, `target-other`
and `target-new`, which are variables like the others:

```
vars.target-here = "i"
vars.target-other = "b"
vars.target-new = "n"

"{panels} l" = { command = "panel:open here metarecord-list" }
"{target-here} {panels} l" = { command = "panel:open here metarecord-list" }
"{target-other} {panels} l" = { command = "panel:open other metarecord-list" }
"{target-new} {panels} l" = { command = "panel:open new metarecord-list" }
```

A command holding `{target}` — the template that once stood for these four lines
— is refused; `metafolder-sync-config` rewrites one left in a file
([[Persisted format changes]]).

!! Changing one of them

The [[keybindings-variables panel]] lists the variables, each with its value, the keys it stands
for and how many lines use it; a new value typed there is written into its line of
the file, and every key built on it moves. Several values are typed apart with ` | `
(`d | x`), and written as a list; a key `|` can be a value only in the file itself. A
new value is refused, naming the variable, when it would bind a key
twice in one place, or make a key alone hide a sequence that nothing hid before — `go`
given the value `x` would hide every `x …` behind the command input's `x`.

The panel lists the ''lines'' of the file: a line using a variable is one row, its key
as written beside the keys it binds. Editing the row edits the line.

A key a variable produced can still be given a meaning of its own: a ''literal'' key of
the file, in the same place, overrides it. Two lines producing the same key in the same
place — two lines with variables, or two literal keys — are refused, as always.

[[Keyboard navigation]] gives the keys as they are shipped, variables expanded,
and the generated notes of each command list its keys the same way.
